-      <p class="why-it-matters">A matrix of pairwise correlations can fail to be a correlation matrix (Definition 1.1) because it can fail to be positive semidefinite (PSD, defined in Lesson 5). The lesson defines the nearest correlation matrix in Frobenius distance (Definitions 1.2 and 2.1) and two repairs: clip-and-rescale (Method 1, Section 4) and alternating projections (Method 2, Section 4). Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, clip-and-rescale gives a correlation matrix when every diagonal entry of the clipped matrix is positive. Of 40 SIMULATED seeded inputs, 23 have a smallest eigenvalue below \(-10^{-9}\); in all 23, Lab 1 finds an alternating-projection output that is a correlation matrix within tolerance and closer to the input than the clip-and-rescale matrix.</p>
+      <p class="why-it-matters">A matrix of pairwise correlations can fail to be a correlation matrix (Definition 1.1), for example because it is not positive semidefinite (PSD, defined in Lesson 5). The lesson defines the nearest correlation matrix in Frobenius distance (Definitions 1.2 and 2.1) and two repairs: clip-and-rescale (Method 1, Section 4) and alternating projections (Method 2, Section 4). Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, clip-and-rescale gives a correlation matrix when every diagonal entry of the clipped matrix is positive. Of 40 SIMULATED seeded inputs, 23 have a smallest eigenvalue below \(-10^{-9}\); in all 23, Lab 1 finds an alternating-projection output that is a correlation matrix within tolerance and closer to the input than the clip-and-rescale matrix.</p>
 
